# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# APEX-OS — CORE image (heavy, slow-moving foundation)
#
#   fedora-bootc:43
#     → CachyOS perf kernel swap + MOK signing                [own RUNs]
#     → out-of-tree modules (nvidia, xone, xpadneo) + MOK     [own RUNs]
#       module signing, NVIDIA userspace, nouveau/KMS config
#     → hardware-enablement firmware                          [own RUN]
#     → desktop + greeter stack (hyprland/quickshell/sway/…)  [own RUN]
#     → sched-ext userspace (scx), Bazaar, RPMFusion + codecs [own RUNs]
#     → baked userland apps, printing, input methods, fonts   [own RUNs]
#     → dev toolchain, Zed, Claude Code, zsh/starship
#     → third-party Rust tools (awww / matugen / yazi)
#     → OS branding, locale, timezone, hardening
#
# ═══ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: `apex update` used to download the whole OS ═══
#
# Everything here used to live in Containerfile.base. That made every published
# image share ONE fate: the base job's path filter fires on `files/**`,
# `apexd/**` and `config/**` — i.e. on almost every commit — and a rebuild with
# no layer cache gives every `dnf`/`RUN` layer a brand-new digest even when the
# installed content is identical. bootc skips a layer only when it already has
# that exact digest, so a one-line shell tweak invalidated all ~90 of our layers
# and `apex update` pulled ~4 GiB. Measured on the author's L16: 155 layers,
# 5.07 GiB compressed, essentially all of it re-fetched every single update.
#
# The fix is structural, not a cache trick. The slow-moving ~95% of the image is
# built HERE, pushed once, and consumed by Containerfile.base as
# `FROM apex-os-core@sha256:…`. Pinning by digest means those layer descriptors
# are REUSED VERBATIM in the derived manifest — bootc already has the blobs and
# fetches none of them. A normal push now rebuilds only the thin base +
# flavor tiers, so `apex update` moves tens of MiB instead of gigabytes.
#
# ── What belongs in core vs base ──
#   CORE  anything installed by dnf, fetched from the network, or compiled;
#         OS-level branding/locale/hardening that does not read files/**;
#         and — since the three published images became one — every out-of-tree
#         KERNEL MODULE, because a module can only be made loadable under an
#         enforcing kernel by signing it with the APEX MOK, and that private key
#         exists only inside a CI build secret that is mounted here.
#   BASE  every COPY out of files/** and config/**, the apexd/apex binaries,
#         the vendored APEX Shell, and any RUN that asserts against them.
# A RUN that mixes the two must be SPLIT, not moved wholesale. Four in the
# original file did exactly that (zsh verification, the Windows-boot helper,
# the Hyprland template guards, the icon-cache/dconf rebuild) and each one is
# now two RUNs, one per tier.
#
# ── Rebuild policy ──
# Core rebuilds when THIS file or kernel/** changes, when forced, or when the
# weekly cron finds a new fedora-bootc digest. It deliberately does NOT rebuild
# just because upstream might have moved: the digest of the base it was built
# from is stamped as org.apexos.fedora-bootc.digest and CI compares it, so a
# quiet week costs users nothing. `force_core=true` bypasses that.
#
# ── Layering discipline (M0 spike B finding) ──
# Spike B found that folding the NVIDIA akmod build + the scx install into one
# dnf flow corrupted the sqlite rpmdb baked into that layer. The production
# rule, applied throughout this tree: keep every heavy dnf transaction in its
# OWN `RUN … && dnf5 clean all` layer.
#
# ── Base tag: fedora-bootc:43 ──
# APEX stays on Fedora 43 until the image's kernel, desktop, and installer stack
# are deliberately migrated and tested together. The original solopasha
# Hyprland COPR was retired after M0; sdegler's maintained fork follows Fedora
# branching and currently publishes the complete stack for F43 and F44.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ═══ Tool builder — third-party Rust programs, toolchain kept out of /usr ═══
#   • awww + awww-daemon (the swww fork APEX Shell's WallpaperService drives;
#     not packaged for Fedora). Best-effort: a build failure writes a marker and
#     does NOT abort the image (the swaybg shim below covers it).
#   • matugen (crates.io) — the shell's theming engine. Fatal on failure.
#   • yazi (pinned git workspace) — the TUI file manager. Non-fatal.
#
# apexd/apex are NOT built here. They are the one Rust artefact that changes on
# almost every commit, so they are compiled in Containerfile.base instead —
# keeping this stage, and everything downstream of it, off the hot path.

FROM quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:43 AS toolbuilder
# /root is special in bootc images (symlink → /var/roothome), so cargo's default
# $HOME/.cargo fails ("File exists" on mkdir). Point CARGO_HOME at the build tree.
ENV CARGO_HOME=/build/.cargo
# Parallel RPM downloads (default 3 → 20): base stages are download-bound, not
# CPU-bound, so this is the biggest build-time lever. See apex-logs 38.
# awww build deps (apex-logs 39): its `common` crate links liblz4 via pkg-config
# and awww-daemon needs wayland-protocols/wayland/xkbcommon dev files — without
# these the build-script exits 101 and the wallpaper daemon silently never builds.
# gcc/cc is needed by several -sys crates.
RUN sed -i '/^\[main\]/a max_parallel_downloads=20' /etc/dnf/dnf.conf; \
    dnf5 -y install cargo rust git-core gcc lz4-devel pkgconf-pkg-config \
        wayland-protocols-devel wayland-devel libxkbcommon-devel >/dev/null \
    && dnf5 clean all

# `mkdir -p /out` first, and it is load-bearing rather than tidy. Nothing else
# creates /out: on the success path `install -D` makes /out/usr/bin as a side
# effect, so the status write that follows happens to work. On the FAILURE path
# nothing has created /out, so `echo … > /out/awww-status` fails with "No such
# file or directory" and `set -e` kills the build — which means the branch
# labelled "non-fatal" was only ever non-fatal when the build had succeeded.
#
# It cost a real build. Codeberg returned 504 during the final image build, the
# clone failed exactly as this branch anticipates, and the step exited 1 anyway.
# Reproduced in a Fedora 43 container before fixing: `if false; then …; else
# echo x > /out/awww-status; fi` under `set -eux` fails on the redirect.
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir -p /out; \
    if git clone --depth 1 https://codeberg.org/LGFae/awww /build/awww \
       && cd /build/awww && cargo build --release; then \
        for b in target/release/awww target/release/awww-daemon; do \
            [ -x "$b" ] && install -Dm755 "$b" "/out/usr/bin/$(basename "$b")"; \
        done; echo "awww: built" > /out/awww-status; \
    else echo "awww: BUILD FAILED (wallpaper daemon absent; non-fatal)" > /out/awww-status; \
    fi; cat /out/awww-status
# matugen (Rust, from crates.io) — built here rather than via a COPR that lacks a
# fedora-43 build; COPY'd out with the other binaries. The shell's theming needs
# it, so this one IS fatal on failure.
RUN cargo install matugen --root /out/usr
# yazi (Rust TUI file manager, the CLI companion to Thunar). NOT packaged in
# Fedora dist-git, and `cargo install yazi-fm yazi-cli` from crates.io is BROKEN:
# yazi-plugin `include_bytes!`s its preset .lua files via a workspace-relative
# path (../yazi-plugin/preset/…) that only resolves inside the intact repo tree,
# not when the crates are unpacked individually from the registry. So build from
# the pinned git WORKSPACE (same clone+build pattern as awww above). yazi-fm →
# `yazi`, yazi-cli → `ya`. Non-fatal: a failure leaves the TUI FM absent (Thunar,
# the GUI handler, remains) but never aborts the image.
RUN set -eux; \
    if git clone --depth 1 --branch v26.5.6 https://github.com/sxyazi/yazi /build/yazi \
       && cd /build/yazi \
       && cargo build --release --locked -p yazi-fm -p yazi-cli; then \
        install -Dm755 target/release/yazi /out/usr/bin/yazi; \
        install -Dm755 target/release/ya   /out/usr/bin/ya; \
        echo "yazi: built v26.5.6 from git workspace"; \
    else echo "yazi: BUILD FAILED (TUI file manager absent; non-fatal — Thunar remains)"; fi

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FROM quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:43

# OCI provenance. The git SHA is injected by CI (--build-arg APEX_REVISION=…).
ARG APEX_REVISION=dev
ARG APEX_VERSION=43
# The digest of the fedora-bootc image this core was actually built from. CI
# resolves the live digest of the upstream tag and compares it with the label on
# the published apex-os-core:latest; when they match, the weekly cron SKIPS the
# core rebuild. Without this, every Monday reissued ~5 GiB of byte-different but
# content-identical layers to every user on the fleet. `unknown` (the default,
# i.e. a local build) never matches, so a local build is never mistaken for a
# reusable one.
ARG FEDORA_BOOTC_DIGEST=unknown
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="APEX-OS core" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="APEX-OS slow-moving foundation: fedora-bootc:43 + CachyOS kernel + Hyprland/quickshell + greetd host + scx + Bazaar + codecs + userland" \
      org.opencontainers.image.source="https://github.com/AndreNijman/apex-os" \
      org.opencontainers.image.revision="${APEX_REVISION}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.version="${APEX_VERSION}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.licenses="MIT" \
      org.opencontainers.image.vendor="APEX-OS" \
      org.apexos.fedora-bootc.digest="${FEDORA_BOOTC_DIGEST}"

# ── dnf: parallel downloads (build-speed lever) ────────────────────────────
# Raise max_parallel_downloads from the default 3 → 20 before any dnf runs. The
# heavy base stages are dominated by RPM *download* time, not CPU (a 16-core box
# sits near-idle during them), so more parallel fetches is the single biggest
# build-time win. This persists in the shipped /etc/dnf/dnf.conf, so the Daily/
# Gaming derivatives inherit it AND runtime `dnf`/`rpm-ostree` fetches are faster
# too. (apex-logs 38-dnf-parallel-downloads.md)
RUN sed -i '/^\[main\]/a max_parallel_downloads=20' /etc/dnf/dnf.conf

# ── Stage 1: CachyOS kernel swap ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Replace Fedora's kernel with the CachyOS-for-Fedora COPR kernel (BORE +
# sched-ext, 1000 Hz) — the Phase-A perf kernel proven in M0 spike B.
# `-devel-matched` pulls the version-locked headers the Gaming edition needs to
# build the out-of-tree NVIDIA akmod against exactly this kernel. The COPRs are
# disabled again so the shipped image carries no active COPR repo (bootc
# hygiene); the RPMs are baked in. Own transaction, `dnf5 clean all` at the end.
#
# The kernel install is split so the kernel-cachyos-core %posttrans can fail
# cosmetically without aborting the build: on F43 that scriptlet runs
# rpm-ostree's kernel-install → dracut BEFORE `modules.dep` is written, so
# dracut errors "modules.dep is missing" and the scriptlet returns non-zero
# (on F42/spike B it merely warned). The RPMs are fully installed regardless
# (posttrans is the final phase) — we regenerate the initramfs ourselves in the
# next step. So: tolerate the install's non-zero exit, then HARD-GATE on
# `rpm -q` proving the kernel packages actually landed. A genuine failure
# (COPR down, package missing) fails the `rpm -q` and aborts.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install dnf5-plugins; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable bieszczaders/kernel-cachyos; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable bieszczaders/kernel-cachyos-addons; \
    dnf5 -y remove --no-autoremove \
        kernel kernel-core kernel-modules kernel-modules-core; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        kernel-cachyos kernel-cachyos-core kernel-cachyos-modules \
        kernel-cachyos-devel-matched \
      || echo "WARN: kernel install exited non-zero (expected: cosmetic %posttrans dracut) — verifying RPMs landed"; \
    rpm -q kernel-cachyos kernel-cachyos-core kernel-cachyos-modules kernel-cachyos-devel-matched; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable bieszczaders/kernel-cachyos; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable bieszczaders/kernel-cachyos-addons; \
    dnf5 clean all

# Ensure the kernel image + initramfs live under /usr/lib/modules/<kver>/ —
# bootc boots from there, NOT from /boot. Run depmod (modules.dep is present
# post-transaction, but re-assert it) and regenerate the initramfs with a
# generic (non-hostonly) dracut, since the %posttrans one did not complete.
# Then HARD-VERIFY both vmlinuz and initramfs exist. Stamp the kver to
# /usr/lib/apex-kver so the Gaming akmod stage can read it.
#
# ── Serial console: OPT-IN, not default ────────────────────────────────────
# This image previously shipped `console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0` to every
# install. That karg exists for headless QEMU / CI observability, and it is the
# wrong default for a distro strangers install on arbitrary laptops:
#
#   * On any machine whose firmware exposes a UART (docking stations, many
#     business laptops, most desktop boards), the kernel really does route
#     printk to it and every boot pays a 115200-baud serialisation cost.
#   * systemd's getty generator spawns serial-getty@ttyS0 off the karg; the
#     unit is masked below, but that is papering over a karg we should not be
#     setting on user hardware in the first place.
#   * The benefit is CI-only and the cost on unknown hardware cannot be
#     verified from here — so the default must be "off".
#
# CI opts back in with `--build-arg APEX_SERIAL_CONSOLE=1`. The
# serial-getty@ttyS0 mask stays UNCONDITIONAL further down: it is free
# insurance for users whose firmware exposes a UART for other reasons.
ARG APEX_SERIAL_CONSOLE=0
RUN set -eux; \
    KVER="$(basename "$(ls -d /usr/lib/modules/*cachyos*)")"; \
    echo "CachyOS kver: ${KVER}"; \
    depmod -a "${KVER}"; \
    if [ ! -s "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/initramfs.img" ]; then \
        echo "initramfs missing -> regenerating with dracut"; \
        dracut --force --no-hostonly --reproducible --zstd \
            --kver "${KVER}" "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/initramfs.img"; \
    fi; \
    test -s "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/initramfs.img"; \
    test -s "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/vmlinuz"; \
    printf '%s\n' "${KVER}" > /usr/lib/apex-kver; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d; \
    if [ "${APEX_SERIAL_CONSOLE}" = "1" ]; then \
        printf 'kargs = ["console=ttyS0,115200", "console=tty0"]\n' \
            > /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/10-apex-serial.toml; \
        echo "serial console: ENABLED (APEX_SERIAL_CONSOLE=1 — CI/QEMU build)"; \
    else \
        rm -f /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/10-apex-serial.toml; \
        echo "serial console: disabled (default; pass --build-arg APEX_SERIAL_CONSOLE=1 for CI)"; \
    fi

# ── Stage 1c: hardware-enablement firmware Fedora's weak deps DO NOT pull ──
# THE BUG THIS FIXES: `linux-firmware` Recommends exactly 13 subpackages
# (amd-gpu, amd-ucode, atheros, brcmfmac, cirrus-audio, intel-audio, intel-gpu,
# mt7xxx, nvidia-gpu, nxpwireless, qcom-wwan, realtek, tiwilink) and NOTHING
# recommends the rest. Fedora ships the remainder only through the
# @hardware-support COMPS GROUP, which this image never installs because it
# installs individual packages. Verified against fedora:43:
#   `dnf repoquery --recommends linux-firmware`  → the 13 above, and no more
#   `dnf group info hardware-support`            → the full default list
#   `dnf repoquery --whatrecommends alsa-sof-firmware` → EMPTY
# and verified against the built image:
#   `find /usr/lib/firmware -name 'iwlwifi*'` → 0 files
#   `find /usr/lib/firmware -iname '*sof*'`   → 0 files
#   /usr/share/alsa/ucm2                      → did not exist
# (note the path: modern iwlwifi firmware lives under intel/iwlwifi/, not at
# the top level — check both before concluding anything about this.)
#
# What that meant for anyone who is not the developer:
#   * iwlwifi-{dvm,mvm,mld}-firmware — Intel Wi-Fi. mvm alone covers 7260
#     through AX211, i.e. the single most common laptop Wi-Fi family there is;
#     mld covers Wi-Fi 7 (BE200/BE201). Without them the driver probes, finds
#     no firmware and the machine has NO WIRELESS NETWORKING. On a laptop with
#     no Ethernet port that is an unrecoverable first boot.
#   * alsa-sof-firmware — the Sound Open Firmware DSP images every Intel
#     laptop since ~Tiger Lake and every AMD ACP laptop (Rembrandt/Phoenix)
#     needs. Without it there is NO AUDIO AT ALL, not even a fallback card.
#   * alsa-ucm — the UCM2 use-case profiles ALSA/PipeWire need to route those
#     codecs. /usr/share/alsa/ucm2 did not exist in the image. Nothing in
#     Fedora recommends it either (verified: --whatrecommends is empty).
#   * intel-vsc-firmware — the Visual Sensing Controller on newer Intel
#     laptops; without it the MIPI/IPU6 webcam never enumerates.
#   * usb_modeswitch — flips USB WWAN/LTE sticks out of their fake-CD mode.
#
# Installed STRICT (no --skip-unavailable): every name here was verified to
# resolve in fedora:43, and a firmware package silently vanishing is exactly
# the class of regression that shipped a no-Wi-Fi image. The long tail of
# legacy/rare radios below is best-effort — cheap to carry, never worth
# aborting a build for. Own transaction, `dnf5 clean all`, per the layering
# discipline at the top of this file.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        alsa-sof-firmware alsa-ucm alsa-utils \
        iwlwifi-dvm-firmware iwlwifi-mvm-firmware iwlwifi-mld-firmware \
        iwlegacy-firmware intel-vsc-firmware \
        libertas-firmware b43-fwcutter b43-openfwwf \
        usb_modeswitch usb_modeswitch-data; \
    dnf5 -y install --skip-unavailable \
        mediatek-firmware qcom-firmware qcom-accel-firmware \
        atmel-firmware zd1211-firmware; \
    test -n "$(find /usr/lib/firmware -name 'iwlwifi*' -print -quit)" \
        || { echo "FATAL: no iwlwifi firmware landed — Intel Wi-Fi would be dead"; exit 1; }; \
    test -d /usr/lib/firmware/intel/sof \
        || { echo "FATAL: no SOF firmware — laptop audio would be dead"; exit 1; }; \
    test -d /usr/share/alsa/ucm2 \
        || { echo "FATAL: no ALSA UCM2 profiles — codec routing would be broken"; exit 1; }; \
    echo "firmware: iwlwifi $(find /usr/lib/firmware -name 'iwlwifi*' | wc -l) files, SOF + UCM2 present"; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 1b: Secure Boot — sign the kernel with the APEX MOK ──────────────
# The CachyOS kernel comes from a COPR and is UNSIGNED (verified: PE certificate
# table size 0). Under Secure Boot, Fedora's signed grub refuses to boot it, so
# APEX would only run with SB disabled. Microsoft will not sign a custom kernel,
# so the standard answer is a Machine Owner Key: we sign vmlinuz ourselves and
# the user enrols our public cert once via MokManager (shim's mechanism).
#
# The private key is supplied as a BUILD SECRET and never enters the image or the
# repo:  podman build --secret id=apex_sb_key,src=/path/apex-mok.key \
#                     --secret id=apex_sb_crt,src=/path/apex-mok.crt
# Without the secret the kernel is left unsigned and the image simply requires
# SB off — the build says so loudly rather than silently shipping something that
# will not boot.
RUN --mount=type=secret,id=apex_sb_key --mount=type=secret,id=apex_sb_crt \
    set -eux; \
    KVER="$(cat /usr/lib/apex-kver)"; \
    if [ -s /run/secrets/apex_sb_key ] && [ -s /run/secrets/apex_sb_crt ]; then \
        dnf5 -y install sbsigntools >/dev/null; \
        sbsign --key /run/secrets/apex_sb_key --cert /run/secrets/apex_sb_crt \
               --output "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/vmlinuz.signed" \
               "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/vmlinuz"; \
        mv "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/vmlinuz.signed" "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/vmlinuz"; \
        sbverify --list "/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}/vmlinuz" | head -5; \
        mkdir -p /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot; \
        openssl x509 -in /run/secrets/apex_sb_crt -outform DER \
                -out /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/apex-mok.der; \
        printf 'signed\n' > /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/kernel-signed; \
        dnf5 -y remove sbsigntools >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; \
        dnf5 clean all; \
        echo "SECURE BOOT: kernel ${KVER} signed with the APEX MOK"; \
    else \
        mkdir -p /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot; \
        printf 'unsigned\n' > /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/kernel-signed; \
        echo "SECURE BOOT: no signing secret supplied — kernel left UNSIGNED (Secure Boot must be OFF)"; \
    fi

# ── Stage 1d: out-of-tree kernel modules — built AND SIGNED here ───────────
#
# WHY THESE ARE IN CORE AND NOT IN A FLAVOR
# APEX publishes ONE image for every laptop. Userspace can be added to a running
# machine (`apex install` builds a systemd system extension); a KERNEL MODULE
# cannot, because a module the kernel will accept has to be signed with the APEX
# MOK private key, and that key is a CI secret that must never reach a user's
# machine. `apex-pkg` refuses kernels/akmods for exactly this reason. So the rule
# that decides what ships and what installs on demand is simply:
#
#     kernel module  -> built and signed HERE, in the image
#     userspace      -> `apex install steam gamescope …` on demand
#
# and everything below is on the kernel-module side of that line.
#
# WHY CORE SPECIFICALLY, GIVEN THE HEADER'S UPDATE-SIZE RULE
# This is the tier the signing secret is already mounted in (Stage 1b), the tier
# that owns the kernel these modules must match, and — decisively — the tier the
# base consumes as `FROM …@sha256:…`. A digest-pinned parent's layer descriptors
# are reused verbatim, so bootc already holds these blobs and downloads none of
# them on a normal `apex update`. Put the same content in `base` or a flavor and
# every user would re-download the NVIDIA driver on every push.
#
# THE SIGNING GAP THIS CLOSES
# Nothing in this repository has ever signed an out-of-tree module. The akmod was
# built in Containerfile.gaming, which is a FLAVOR — and the flavor job is never
# passed `--secret id=apex_sb_key`, so `sign-file` could not have run there even
# if someone had written it. CI's Secure Boot check ran `sbverify` against
# vmlinuz only. AGENTS.md has always required that "the shipped kernel and
# required out-of-tree modules must be signed by the expected chain".
#
# Measured caveat, recorded so nobody over-claims this fix: on the shipped
# CachyOS kernel the modules were LOADING regardless, because that kernel does
# not enforce module signatures —
#     CONFIG_MODULE_SIG=y            (signatures are understood)
#     # CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_FORCE is not set
#     CONFIG_LOCK_DOWN_KERNEL_FORCE_NONE=y
#     CONFIG_LOCK_DOWN_IN_EFI_SECURE_BOOT — absent entirely
# so, unlike a Fedora kernel, Secure Boot does NOT put this kernel into lockdown
# and an unsigned module loads fine today. What the signature buys is that the
# moment enforcement appears — a CachyOS respin enabling the lockdown coupling, a
# user adding `module.sig_enforce=1` or `lockdown=integrity` — NVIDIA keeps
# working instead of silently failing to load into a black screen. And it can be
# trusted when it happens: the kernel carries
#     CONFIG_INTEGRITY_MACHINE_KEYRING=y  +  CONFIG_SECONDARY_TRUSTED_KEYRING=y
# so the MOK the user already enrols for the kernel validates these modules too.
#
# WHAT IS BUILT
#   nvidia    RPMFusion akmod-nvidia (580.x). The dGPU driver.
#   xone      ublue-os/akmods — Xbox One/Series controllers, wired and via the
#             Microsoft wireless dongle. NOTE: the DONGLE also needs a firmware
#             blob extracted from a Microsoft driver package, which is not
#             redistributable and therefore not baked in; wired pads work as
#             shipped, dongle users run `xone-get-firmware.sh` once.
#   xpadneo   shdwchn10/xpadneo 0.10.2 — Bluetooth Xbox pads: rumble, battery
#             reporting and correct axis mapping that in-kernel `hid-generic`
#             does not provide.
# Containerfile.gaming claimed "no akmod source is currently published that
# matches this kernel". That was wrong, and it is worth naming why: an akmod
# source package is kernel-INDEPENDENT — `akmods` compiles it against whatever
# `--kernels` is given. Verified with the COPRs enabled against fc43:
#     akmod-xone     1000.0.0.git.1442.85e53359-2.fc43  [ublue-os/akmods]
#     akmod-xpadneo  0.10.2-1.fc43                      [shdwchn10/xpadneo]
#
# RPMFusion is enabled HERE (earliest consumer) and left enabled — Stage 5a-codecs
# below now asserts it rather than re-adding it. The two akmod COPRs are one-shot
# build inputs and are disabled again, matching the CachyOS kernel COPR posture.
#
# Build and signing are ONE layer on purpose: signing rewrites every module, so
# splitting them would commit the unsigned copies to a lower layer and ship both.
ARG APEX_AKMODS="nvidia xone xpadneo"
RUN --mount=type=secret,id=apex_sb_key --mount=type=secret,id=apex_sb_crt \
    set -eux; \
    KVER="$(cat /usr/lib/apex-kver)"; \
    echo "building out-of-tree modules against CachyOS kver: ${KVER}"; \
    test -d "/usr/src/kernels/${KVER}" \
        || { echo "FATAL: kernel-devel headers missing for ${KVER}"; exit 1; }; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm" \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/nonfree/fedora/rpmfusion-nonfree-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm"; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable ublue-os/akmods; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable shdwchn10/xpadneo; \
    dnf5 -y install akmods kmodtool gcc make; \
    dnf5 -y install akmod-nvidia akmod-xone akmod-xpadneo; \
    for k in ${APEX_AKMODS}; do \
        echo "=== akmods: building ${k} for ${KVER} ==="; \
        akmods --force --kernels "${KVER}" --kmod "${k}"; \
        RPM="$(ls -1 /var/cache/akmods/${k}/kmod-${k}-*.rpm 2>/dev/null | head -1)"; \
        test -n "${RPM}" && test -f "${RPM}" \
            || { echo "FATAL: ${k} akmod produced no kmod RPM for ${KVER}"; \
                 sed -n '1,80p' /var/cache/akmods/${k}/*.failed.log 2>/dev/null; exit 1; }; \
        echo "built: ${RPM}"; \
        dnf5 -y install "${RPM}"; \
    done; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable ublue-os/akmods; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable shdwchn10/xpadneo; \
    depmod -a "${KVER}"; \
    MODDIR="/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}"; \
    SIGN_FILE="${MODDIR}/build/scripts/sign-file"; \
    HASH="$(sed -n 's/^CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_HASH="\(.*\)"$/\1/p' "${MODDIR}/config")"; \
    test -n "${HASH}" \
        || { echo "FATAL: cannot read CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_HASH from ${MODDIR}/config"; exit 1; }; \
    mkdir -p /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot; \
    if [ -s /run/secrets/apex_sb_key ] && [ -s /run/secrets/apex_sb_crt ]; then \
        test -x "${SIGN_FILE}" \
            || { echo "FATAL: ${SIGN_FILE} missing — kernel-devel did not ship the signing helper"; exit 1; }; \
        dnf5 -y install zstd xz >/dev/null; \
        openssl x509 -in /run/secrets/apex_sb_crt -outform DER -out /tmp/apex-mok.der; \
        SIGNER="$(openssl x509 -in /run/secrets/apex_sb_crt -noout -subject -nameopt multiline \
                  | sed -n 's/^ *commonName *= *//p')"; \
        test -n "${SIGNER}" \
            || { echo "FATAL: APEX MOK certificate has no commonName to verify against"; exit 1; }; \
        echo "signing out-of-tree modules with ${HASH}, signer CN='${SIGNER}'"; \
        SIGNED=0; \
        OOT=""; for d in extra updates; do [ -d "${MODDIR}/${d}" ] && OOT="${OOT} ${MODDIR}/${d}"; done; \
        test -n "${OOT}" \
            || { echo "FATAL: no out-of-tree module directory under ${MODDIR}"; exit 1; }; \
        for m in $(find ${OOT} -type f -name '*.ko*'); do \
            case "${m}" in \
                *.ko.zst) zstd -q -d --rm "${m}"; ko="${m%.zst}"; recomp=zst ;; \
                *.ko.xz)  xz -q -d "${m}";        ko="${m%.xz}";  recomp=xz  ;; \
                *.ko)     ko="${m}";              recomp=none ;; \
                *) echo "FATAL: unhandled module file ${m}"; exit 1 ;; \
            esac; \
            "${SIGN_FILE}" "${HASH}" /run/secrets/apex_sb_key /tmp/apex-mok.der "${ko}"; \
            tail -c 28 "${ko}" | grep -q 'Module signature appended' \
                || { echo "FATAL: no signature trailer on ${ko}"; exit 1; }; \
            case "${recomp}" in \
                zst) zstd -q -19 -T0 --rm "${ko}" ;; \
                xz)  xz -q -T0 "${ko}" ;; \
            esac; \
            SIGNED=$((SIGNED + 1)); \
        done; \
        rm -f /tmp/apex-mok.der; \
        test "${SIGNED}" -gt 0 \
            || { echo "FATAL: signed 0 modules — the enumeration found nothing to sign"; exit 1; }; \
        depmod -a "${KVER}"; \
        printf '%s\n' "${SIGNER}" > /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/module-signer; \
        printf 'signed\n' > /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/modules-signed; \
        echo "SECURE BOOT: ${SIGNED} out-of-tree modules signed with the APEX MOK"; \
    else \
        printf 'unsigned\n' > /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/modules-signed; \
        echo "SECURE BOOT: no signing secret — out-of-tree modules left UNSIGNED"; \
    fi; \
    dnf5 clean all

# THE POSTCONDITION. Separate RUN so it cannot share a shell with the code that
# produced the thing it checks, and written so it CANNOT PASS VACUOUSLY: it
# enumerates every out-of-tree module, requires at least one nvidia + one xone +
# one xpadneo, and requires `modinfo -F signer` on each to equal the certificate
# CN recorded above. A marker file is not evidence; this reads the signature out
# of the shipped artifact. `modules-signed = unsigned` (a local build with no
# key) is tolerated here in the same way Stage 1b tolerates an unsigned kernel —
# and CI refuses to publish either.
#
# Module NAMES, not package names: `akmod-xone` builds xone-wired/xone-dongle/
# xone-gip*, and `akmod-xpadneo` builds hid-xpadneo. Only nvidia is named after
# its akmod, so presence is checked by locating the module FILES each akmod
# installed rather than by `modinfo <akmod name>`, which would fail for two of
# the three and would have to be weakened to pass — the exact shape of check
# that stops meaning anything.
RUN set -eux; \
    KVER="$(cat /usr/lib/apex-kver)"; \
    MODDIR="/usr/lib/modules/${KVER}"; \
    OOT=""; for d in extra updates; do [ -d "${MODDIR}/${d}" ] && OOT="${OOT} ${MODDIR}/${d}"; done; \
    test -n "${OOT}" || { echo "FATAL: no out-of-tree module directory"; exit 1; }; \
    for pat in nvidia xone xpadneo; do \
        n="$(find ${OOT} -type f -name "*${pat}*.ko*" | wc -l)"; \
        test "${n}" -gt 0 \
            || { echo "FATAL: no ${pat} module under ${OOT}"; find ${OOT} -name '*.ko*'; exit 1; }; \
        echo "present: ${n} ${pat} module(s)"; \
    done; \
    modinfo -k "${KVER}" nvidia >/dev/null \
        || { echo "FATAL: modinfo cannot resolve nvidia for ${KVER}"; exit 1; }; \
    if [ "$(cat /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/modules-signed)" = signed ]; then \
        SIGNER="$(cat /usr/share/apex-os/secureboot/module-signer)"; \
        N=0; \
        for m in $(find ${OOT} -type f -name '*.ko*'); do \
            got="$(modinfo -F signer "${m}" 2>/dev/null || true)"; \
            [ "${got}" = "${SIGNER}" ] \
                || { echo "FATAL: ${m} signer is '${got}', expected '${SIGNER}'"; exit 1; }; \
            N=$((N + 1)); \
        done; \
        test "${N}" -gt 0 \
            || { echo "FATAL: zero out-of-tree modules found — this check would have passed vacuously"; exit 1; }; \
        echo "module signatures verified: ${N} modules, all signed by '${SIGNER}'"; \
    else \
        echo "modules are UNSIGNED (no build secret); CI will refuse to publish this"; \
    fi

# ── Stage 1e: NVIDIA userspace driver (every image, every machine) ─────────
# The stage above built the kernel MODULE; a working NVIDIA machine also needs
# the USERSPACE driver (libGLX_nvidia / libnvidia-*) plus the CUDA runtime. The
# VA-API↔NVDEC bridge (RPMFusion renamed nvidia-vaapi-driver →
# libva-nvidia-driver) gives the codec userland hardware decode on NVIDIA too;
# best-effort across BOTH names with `--skip-unavailable`, so a rename upstream
# can never abort the driver + CUDA install that matters.
#
# COEXISTENCE WITH MESA IS THE WHOLE QUESTION HERE, because this now lands on
# AMD-only and Intel-only laptops too. It is safe because Fedora/RPMFusion ship
# GLVND: mesa provides libGLX_mesa/libEGL_mesa, NVIDIA provides
# libGLX_nvidia/libEGL_nvidia, and libglvnd dispatches per-screen from the
# vendor JSON in /usr/share/glvnd/egl_vendor.d — there is no libGL.so
# "alternative" to win or lose any more, which is what the old
# `nvidia-xconfig`-era conflict was about. Asserted, not assumed: the next RUN
# checks both vendors' GLX/EGL libraries and Vulkan ICDs survive this install.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install xorg-x11-drv-nvidia xorg-x11-drv-nvidia-cuda; \
    dnf5 -y install --skip-unavailable libva-nvidia-driver nvidia-vaapi-driver; \
    dnf5 clean all

# Mesa must still work on a machine that has no NVIDIA GPU at all. These are the
# files that make that true, so these are what is checked — the mechanism is
# GLVND dispatch, the evidence is that both vendors are present and neither
# replaced the other.
RUN set -eux; \
    test -e /usr/lib64/libGLX_mesa.so.0; \
    test -e /usr/lib64/libEGL_mesa.so.0; \
    test -e /usr/lib64/libGLX_nvidia.so.0; \
    test -s /usr/share/glvnd/egl_vendor.d/50_mesa.json; \
    test -s /usr/share/glvnd/egl_vendor.d/10_nvidia.json; \
    ls /usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/ | grep -q '^radeon_icd'; \
    ls /usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/ | grep -q '^intel_icd'; \
    ls /usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/ | grep -qi 'nvidia'; \
    # THE ONE THAT MATTERS. The historical Mesa/NVIDIA conflict was over who
    # owned libGL.so.1. Under GLVND neither vendor does — libglvnd owns the
    # dispatch library and both ship only libGLX_<vendor>. If a driver update
    # ever went back to claiming libGL.so.1, an AMD/Intel laptop would be
    # dispatching its OpenGL through the NVIDIA stack, and this is what catches
    # it. Checked by package ownership, not by the file merely existing.
    owner="$(rpm -qf --qf '%{NAME}\n' /usr/lib64/libGL.so.1)"; \
    case "${owner}" in libglvnd*) ;; \
        *) echo "FATAL: /usr/lib64/libGL.so.1 is owned by '${owner}', not libglvnd — mesa/nvidia GL dispatch is broken"; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    echo "glvnd: libGL.so.1 owned by ${owner}; mesa and nvidia GLX/EGL/Vulkan coexist"

# ── Stage 1f: NVIDIA boot correctness — nouveau, KMS, initramfs ────────────
# Everything here MUST land before the LAST initramfs regeneration in the build,
# which is the Plymouth dracut run in Containerfile.apex. (apex-logs 46)
#
# THREE FINDINGS FROM THE FLASH-READINESS REVIEW, unchanged by unification:
#
# 1. nouveau is NOT blacklisted by anything else. RPMFusion ships its blacklist
#    ONLY via a %posttrans grubby call, which is inert on bootc — there is no
#    modprobe.d fallback anywhere in RPMFusion F43. The CachyOS kernel ships
#    nouveau with a catch-all pci:v000010DEd* alias AND nouveau is present in the
#    initramfs, so it races nvidia for the dGPU during early boot.
#
# 2. RPMFusion deliberately OMITS nvidia from the initramfs
#    (/usr/lib/dracut/dracut.conf.d/99-nvidia-dracut.conf), reasoning "avoid
#    needing to regenerate the ramdisk on updates". That reasoning does not apply
#    to bootc, where every driver update IS an image rebuild — and the omission
#    means the splash/greeter cannot come up on a dGPU-attached output. We
#    OVERWRITE that exact file (plain `=`, not `+=`) rather than relying on
#    add_drivers-vs-omit_drivers precedence, so the fix is correct either way.
#
# 3. nvidia-drm modeset: RPMFusion's nvidia-kmod carries make_modeset_default.patch
#    so the built default is already on. We set it EXPLICITLY anyway, so a future
#    RPMFusion respin that drops that patch cannot silently ship an image with no
#    KMS — an explicit modprobe option beats whatever the compiled-in default is.
#
# WHAT UNIFICATION CHANGES, STATED PLAINLY: every APEX machine now carries the
# nouveau blacklist, not just the ex-`gaming-nvidia` ones. On AMD/Intel hardware
# that is inert (no NVIDIA device, nothing to bind). On a machine whose NVIDIA
# GPU is too old for the 580 branch it means the dGPU goes unused rather than
# falling back to nouveau — acceptable on the laptops this image targets, where
# the panel is driven by the iGPU, and preferable to the boot race above.
# The nvidia-*.service condition gates and the nvidia-fallback mask below are
# what keep the image clean on machines with no NVIDIA hardware; they were
# already written for portability and are now load-bearing for everyone.
# printf, not a heredoc: heredoc bodies do not survive backslash-continued RUN
# lines reliably, and this project has already shipped one Dockerfile-escaping bug.
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/modprobe.d /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d /usr/lib/dracut/dracut.conf.d; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '# APEX-OS (apex-logs 46). nouveau must never bind the dGPU: it races' \
        '# the proprietary driver, and RPMFusion ships its blacklist only via a' \
        '# %posttrans grubby call, which is inert on bootc.' \
        'blacklist nouveau' \
        'install nouveau /bin/false' \
        '# Explicit, so we do not depend on RPMFusion make_modeset_default.patch' \
        '# surviving a respin. modeset=1 is required by every Wayland compositor;' \
        '# fbdev=1 gives a console/splash framebuffer on dGPU-attached outputs.' \
        'options nvidia-drm modeset=1 fbdev=1' \
        > /usr/lib/modprobe.d/50-apex-nvidia.conf; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '# rd.driver.blacklist is the mechanism dracut honours in the INITRAMFS' \
        '# phase — modprobe.d alone is weaker there, and nouveau is verifiably' \
        '# inside the initramfs. modprobe.blacklist covers the real root.' \
        'kargs = ["rd.driver.blacklist=nouveau", "modprobe.blacklist=nouveau"]' \
        > /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/25-apex-nvidia.toml; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '# APEX-OS overwrite of RPMFusion 99-nvidia-dracut.conf.' \
        '# Assignment, NOT +=, so their omit_drivers is cleared outright and we' \
        '# do not depend on add_drivers-vs-omit_drivers precedence.' \
        'omit_drivers=""' \
        'add_drivers+=" nvidia nvidia_modeset nvidia_drm nvidia_uvm "' \
        > /usr/lib/dracut/dracut.conf.d/99-nvidia-dracut.conf; \
    systemctl mask nvidia-fallback.service; \
    for u in nvidia-powerd nvidia-persistenced; do \
        mkdir -p "/usr/lib/systemd/system/${u}.service.d"; \
        printf '%s\n' \
            '# Portability (apex-logs 46): this image runs on ANY machine,' \
            '# including ones with no NVIDIA GPU. Without this gate these units' \
            '# fail at every boot on AMD/Intel hardware and show up as failed' \
            '# units, which is noise the user then has to triage.' \
            '[Unit]' \
            'ConditionPathExistsGlob=/sys/module/nvidia' \
            > "/usr/lib/systemd/system/${u}.service.d/10-apex-nvidia-only.conf"; \
    done; \
    test -s /usr/lib/modprobe.d/50-apex-nvidia.conf; \
    test -s /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/25-apex-nvidia.toml; \
    grep -q 'omit_drivers=""' /usr/lib/dracut/dracut.conf.d/99-nvidia-dracut.conf; \
    echo "nvidia: nouveau blacklisted, KMS forced, driver added to initramfs"

# ── Stage 1g: controller + fan/IRQ policy that is no longer edition-specific ─
# These four moved out of Containerfile.gaming unchanged in effect. They were
# never gaming-only in nature — they are hardware and daemon-arbitration policy —
# and with one image there is no edition to gate them on.
#
# msi-wmi-platform: the in-tree msi-ec has a 25-entry EC-firmware allowlist with
# NO 17L3 entry and no `force` parameter, so it will NOT bind on the MSI Katana
# GF76 (board MS-17L3). msi-wmi-platform does take force=1 and registers an hwmon
# device, so it is the viable fan/telemetry backend there. Loading it taints the
# kernel; harmless, and a no-op on non-MSI hardware where the WMI GUID is absent.
# apexd probes hwmon -> msi-wmi-platform -> msi-ec -> unsupported.
#
# irqbalance is MASKED (it is installed, as a dependency, on every image — so the
# `|| true` this carried in Containerfile.gaming was dead weight guarding a
# command that cannot fail, and is gone). It actively undoes apexd's M6 IRQ
# pinning and a static IRQBALANCE_BANNED_CPULIST cannot track a cpuset chosen at
# runtime. On a laptop the cost of not running it is negligible; the cost of it
# fighting apexd is a measurable frame-pacing regression.
#
# /etc/gamemode.ini ships even though gamemode itself is now an on-demand
# package: it is inert without gamemode, and it means the FIRST time a user runs
# `apex install gamemode`, gamemoded already hands the governor to apexd instead
# of flapping it. Config that arrives after the daemon is config nobody applies.
#
# xone/xpadneo module options: xpadneo's rumble and the xone dongle are what the
# akmods above exist for; these give both sane defaults without a udev rule.
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/modules-load.d /usr/lib/modprobe.d; \
    printf '%s\n' 'msi-wmi-platform' > /usr/lib/modules-load.d/apex-msi.conf; \
    printf '%s\n' 'options msi-wmi-platform force=1' \
        > /usr/lib/modprobe.d/51-apex-msi.conf; \
    systemctl mask irqbalance.service; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '[general]' \
        '; apexd owns scaling_governor (M6 tier engine). Matching gamemoded to' \
        '; apexd game tier means neither daemon flaps the governor against the' \
        '; other.' \
        'desiredgov=performance' \
        'defaultgov=performance' \
        'igpu_desiredgov=performance' \
        '' \
        '[custom]' \
        '; Makes Steam `gamemoderun %command%` drive APEX game mode: P-core' \
        '; cpuset, IRQ steering, NVIDIA clock locks and fan boost. Idempotent,' \
        '; and safe when apexd is absent (the CLI reports unsupported, not err).' \
        'start=/usr/bin/apex game start' \
        'end=/usr/bin/apex game stop' \
        > /etc/gamemode.ini; \
    test -s /usr/lib/modules-load.d/apex-msi.conf; \
    test -s /usr/lib/modprobe.d/51-apex-msi.conf; \
    test -s /etc/gamemode.ini; \
    test "$(readlink -f /etc/systemd/system/irqbalance.service)" = /dev/null \
        || { echo "FATAL: irqbalance.service is not masked"; exit 1; }

# ── Stage 2: desktop + greeter stack ───────────────────────────────────────
# hyprland (sdegler COPR) + quickshell (errornointernet COPR 0.3.0 —
# the exact version apex-greet targets) + greetd. The greeter's compositor host
# is moving OFF cage: M0 spike A found cage 0.2.0 does not serve wlr-layer-shell
# to quickshell 0.3.0, so the greeter never paints under it. sway 1.11 and labwc
# 0.9.6 (both stock F43, both layer-shell v4) are installed as the real host;
# cage is kept only as a last-resort fallback. xwayland-satellite/foot round out
# the session. flatpak + distrobox are the userspace app plumbing both editions
# want (podman + rpm-ostree are already in fedora-bootc). COPRs disabled after.
#
# glibc-langpack-en + zram-generator-defaults close two first-boot gaps found on
# the p6 install (apex-logs 53):
#   • glibc-langpack-en — the image shipped ONLY glibc-minimal-langpack, so
#     `locale -a` listed nothing but C/C.utf8/POSIX and en_US.UTF-8 did not
#     EXIST on the system. The installer kickstart asks for `lang en_US.UTF-8`
#     and `localectl set-locale LANG=en_US.UTF-8` looks like it works, but
#     neither can be satisfied without this package — so every install fell back
#     to the systemd default C.UTF-8 (no collation rules, C-style time/number
#     formatting).
#   • zram-generator-defaults — zram-generator itself was already pulled in as a
#     dependency, but with NO configuration, so the generator ran and created
#     nothing. Result: no swap of any kind, and `systemd-oomd` logging
#     "No swap; memory pressure usage will be degraded" on every boot, which
#     leaves OOM handling abrupt instead of graduated. This package ships
#     Fedora's standard /usr/lib/systemd/zram-generator.conf (min(ram/2, 8G),
#     zstd) — the same default the other Fedora atomic desktops carry.
#
# kanshi + wlr-randr are the display-settings backend. wlr-randr applies a
# change live; kanshi holds the persistent per-arrangement profiles and reapplies
# them on hotplug. Both speak wlr-output-management, so they serve the labwc and
# niri sessions directly; Hyprland does not implement that protocol and is driven
# through hyprctl by the same APEX settings model.
#
# wlopm is DISPLAY POWER, which is a different protocol and a different job from
# wlr-randr's. It is the only thing that can turn the screen off on labwc:
# labwc publishes no IPC socket at all, so there is no `hyprctl dispatch dpms`
# or `niri msg action power-off-monitors` equivalent, and the one protocol it
# does implement for this is zwlr-output-power-management-v1 (confirmed:
# `strings /usr/bin/labwc` contains zwlr_output_power_manager_v1). Without it
# the idle screen-off listener in the shipped hypridle.conf fired on labwc and
# did nothing at all — the display stayed lit all night.
#
# wlr-randr is NOT a substitute. `--off` goes through wlr-output-management and
# DISABLES the output: layer-shell surfaces on it are destroyed and windows are
# re-laid-out onto whatever is left, every single idle timeout. That is worse
# than the no-op it would replace. 28 KiB, and it is the whole fix.
#
# bubblewrap is the agent sandbox. It was already present as a flatpak
# dependency and Containerfile.base asserts it, deliberately, so that the
# security boundary did not force a core rebuild on its own. This transaction is
# rebuilding core anyway for kanshi, so the dependency is now explicit at no
# additional cost — a security primitive should not rest on another package
# happening to pull it in. The base assertion stays: it is what catches the
# package being dropped again.
# Own transaction, `dnf5 clean all` at the end.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable sdegler/hyprland; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable errornointernet/quickshell; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        hyprland quickshell greetd sway labwc cage xwayland-satellite foot \
        jetbrains-mono-fonts fontconfig flatpak distrobox git-core niri \
        hyprlock hypridle hyprsunset hyprshot grimblast mate-polkit \
        pipewire pipewire-pulseaudio wireplumber rtkit \
        playerctl brightnessctl wl-clipboard slurp wtype cliphist \
        upower libnotify lm_sensors cava wf-recorder ImageMagick \
        qt6ct qt6-qtwebengine mpv-mpris xdg-user-dirs polkit gnome-keyring \
        gammastep geoclue2 jq dconf \
        kanshi wlr-randr wlopm \
        bubblewrap \
        NetworkManager-wifi wpa_supplicant wireless-regdb iw \
        glibc-langpack-en zram-generator-defaults; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable sdegler/hyprland; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable errornointernet/quickshell; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── hyprland-qtutils, built HERE against the desktop stack ──────────────────
# Hyprland warns at every startup without it, and CAsyncDialogBox cannot create
# dialogs at all — the ANR manager never starts either. hyprland *recommends*
# the packaged build may link against a different Qt PRIVATE API than the image,
# whose private-API symbol versions must match exactly. So it is built from
# source against the Qt version actually shipping here.
#
# It is built in THIS stage, not a separate builder, and that is deliberate. A
# builder starting from bare fedora-bootc resolves hyprutils-devel to FEDORA's
# 0.7.1 (the COPR does not ship that subpackage) while this image runs 0.10.0
# from the COPR — so the build linked against different libraries than the
# target and cmake failed on the Qt private targets. Building where the desktop
# stack already exists guarantees the versions are the ones actually shipping.
#
# Devel packages are installed and removed in the SAME layer, so ~1 GB of Qt
# headers never reaches the image. Pinned to release tags, not main: this runs
# on every base rebuild including the weekly cron, and tracking upstream HEAD
# would let an unrelated commit break everyone's build.
ARG HYPR_QT_SUPPORT_TAG=v0.1.0
# Pinned to a COMMIT, not v0.1.5: that tag predates upstream 5ffdfc1
# "cmake: Added find_package entry for WaylandClientPrivate", without which
# the build fails against Qt 6.10 with "Target ... links to
# Qt6::WaylandClientPrivate but the target was not found". Verified: v0.1.5
# fails and this commit configures cleanly, both against the real desktop stack.
ARG HYPR_QTUTILS_REF=56d993aced40a9058350d24ff4a7fb4eb961f89b
COPY files/system/src/quickshell-argc-shim.c /tmp/quickshell-argc-shim.c
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable sdegler/hyprland; \
    dnf5 -y install cmake ninja-build gcc-c++ git-core pkgconf \
        qt6-qtbase-devel qt6-qtdeclarative-devel qt6-qtsvg-devel \
        qt6-qtwayland-devel qt6-qtbase-private-devel \
        qt6-qtdeclarative-private-devel \
        hyprlang-devel hyprutils-devel; \
    rpm -q hyprutils-devel qt6-qtbase-devel qt6-qtwayland-devel; \
    git clone --depth 1 --branch "${HYPR_QT_SUPPORT_TAG}" \
        https://github.com/hyprwm/hyprland-qt-support /b/qs; \
    cmake -S /b/qs -B /b/qs/build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr -DINSTALL_QML_PREFIX=lib64/qt6/qml; \
    cmake --build /b/qs/build -j"$(nproc)"; \
    cmake --install /b/qs/build; \
    mkdir -p /b/qu; git -C /b/qu init -q; \
    git -C /b/qu remote add origin https://github.com/hyprwm/hyprland-qtutils; \
    git -C /b/qu fetch -q --depth 1 origin "${HYPR_QTUTILS_REF}"; \
    git -C /b/qu checkout -q FETCH_HEAD; \
    cmake -S /b/qu -B /b/qu/build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr; \
    cmake --build /b/qu/build -j"$(nproc)"; \
    cmake --install /b/qu/build; \
    for b in hyprland-dialog hyprland-donate-screen hyprland-update-screen; do \
        command -v "$b" >/dev/null || { echo "FATAL: $b missing"; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    ! ldd /usr/bin/hyprland-dialog | grep 'not found'; \
    test -d /usr/lib64/qt6/qml/org/hyprland/style; \
    test ! -d /usr/usr; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib64/apex; \
    gcc -shared -fPIC -O2 -Wl,-z,relro,-z,now \
        /tmp/quickshell-argc-shim.c -ldl \
        -o /usr/lib64/apex/libapex-quickshell-argc.so; \
    nm -D /usr/lib64/apex/libapex-quickshell-argc.so \
        | grep -q '_ZN15QGuiApplicationC1ERiPPci'; \
    rm -rf /b; \
    rm -f /tmp/quickshell-argc-shim.c; \
    dnf5 -y remove cmake ninja-build gcc-c++ pkgconf \
        'qt6-*-devel' 'hypr*-devel' || true; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable sdegler/hyprland; \
    dnf5 clean all; \
    command -v hyprland-dialog; \
    echo "hyprland-qtutils: built against the shipping Qt and hypr libraries"

# ── Stage 3: JetBrainsMono Nerd Font ───────────────────────────────────────
# apex-greet's QML uses the "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font" family for both body text
# and its Nerd-Font glyphs (person / lock / caps-lock icons). Fedora packages
# only plain JetBrains Mono (no Nerd glyphs), so fetch the patched family. ADD
# downloads but does not auto-extract a tarball, so unpack in the RUN.
ADD https://github.com/ryanoasis/nerd-fonts/releases/download/v3.4.0/JetBrainsMono.tar.xz \
    /usr/share/fonts/jetbrains-mono-nerd/JetBrainsMono.tar.xz
RUN set -eux; \
    cd /usr/share/fonts/jetbrains-mono-nerd; \
    tar -xJf JetBrainsMono.tar.xz; \
    rm -f JetBrainsMono.tar.xz; \
    fc-cache -f /usr/share/fonts/jetbrains-mono-nerd

# ── Stage 4: sched-ext userspace (scx) ─────────────────────────────────────
# scx-scheds (the scheduler set) + scx-tools (scx_loader/scxctl) from the
# CachyOS addons COPR — the sched-ext userspace apexd will drive. Isolated in
# its OWN transaction because M0 spike B traced rpmdb corruption to combining
# this install with the akmod flow. `dnf5 clean all` at the end.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable bieszczaders/kernel-cachyos-addons; \
    dnf5 -y install scx-scheds scx-tools; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable bieszczaders/kernel-cachyos-addons; \
    rpm -q scx-scheds scx-tools > /usr/lib/apex-scx-versions 2>&1 || true; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5: Bazaar app store ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bazaar (leaf GTK4 Flatpak store — the M0 store decision; no gnome-shell /
# PackageKit pulled in) from the ublue-os/packages COPR, the same RPM path
# Bazzite uses. COPR disabled after; own transaction, `dnf5 clean all`.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable ublue-os/packages; \
    dnf5 -y install bazaar; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable ublue-os/packages; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-codecs: RPMFusion + full multimedia stack (§4c) ───────────────
# Fedora ships a legally-crippled multimedia stack: `ffmpeg-free` (no H.264/H.265/
# AAC encoders) and mesa built WITHOUT the proprietary video codecs, so hardware
# H.264/H.265 decode on the AMD 780M is dead out of the box. This stage fixes the
# codec userland AND — new with the single image — the mesa leg itself.
#
# RPMFusion free+nonfree are enabled in Stage 1d (the akmod build is the earliest
# consumer) and LEFT ENABLED, unlike the COPRs, which are one-shot build inputs
# disabled after use: RPMFusion is a persistent, well-known repo the running
# system wants so users can install codecs/drivers — the same posture Bazzite
# ships. Asserted here rather than re-added, so deleting Stage 1d fails loudly
# instead of silently depsolving this stage against Fedora alone.
#
# ── THE MESA LEG, MOVED HERE FROM THE FLAVOR TIER ──
# §4c used to pin Daily to latest-STABLE mesa (Terra) and Gaming to mesa-*git*.
# One image cannot have two, and latest-stable wins on two counts:
#
#   1. mesa-git's entire value is being fresh, and nothing in `core` is fresh —
#      core rebuilds only on an upstream digest change or a force. A git snapshot
#      frozen for weeks at a time is the worst of both: the regression risk of
#      main-branch RADV with none of the currency that was the point.
#   2. This image is now the one every laptop runs, including the ones whose
#      acceptance gate is suspend reliability and idle drain. Shipping
#      main-branch Mesa to all of them to chase frame pacing on some of them is
#      the wrong trade. `apex repo enable-copr xxmitsu/mesa-git` remains for
#      anyone who wants it per-machine.
#
# Terra (fyralabs) ships a newer *release* mesa than the fc43-pinned base, built
# WITHOUT Fedora's codec crippling — so one distro-sync gets BOTH the fresher
# stable stack AND H.264/H.265 hardware decode on RADV (the point of the base's
# mpv.conf `hwdec=auto-safe`), with no separate freeworld swap.
#
# The Terra repo is REMOVED after the sync. It must NOT stay enabled in the
# shipped image: terra-release's repo file points gpgkey at
# file:///etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-terra$releasever, and any later depsolve
# against that enabled repo (bootc-image-builder's ISO build, or an `apex
# install`) fails to read the key and aborts — which is exactly what broke the
# first installer-ISO build. Falls back to Fedora-stable mesa + RPMFusion
# freeworld VA if Terra is unavailable at build time; codecs work either way.
#
# Swap ffmpeg-free → full ffmpeg with --allowerasing (they conflict), then the
# GStreamer plugin set (good is Fedora; bad-freeworld/ugly/libav are RPMFusion) +
# VA-API userland. openh264 comes from Fedora's Cisco repo. Own transaction.
RUN set -eux; \
    rpm -q rpmfusion-free-release rpmfusion-nonfree-release >/dev/null \
        || { echo "FATAL: RPMFusion is not enabled — Stage 1d must run before this stage"; exit 1; }; \
    dnf5 -y install --allowerasing ffmpeg; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        gstreamer1-plugins-base gstreamer1-plugins-good \
        gstreamer1-plugins-bad-free gstreamer1-plugins-bad-freeworld \
        gstreamer1-plugins-ugly gstreamer1-libav \
        libva libva-utils; \
    dnf5 -y install --skip-unavailable gstreamer1-vaapi; \
    if dnf5 -y install --nogpgcheck \
            --repofrompath "terra,https://repos.fyralabs.com/terra\$releasever" \
            terra-release \
       && dnf5 -y distro-sync "mesa*"; then \
        dnf5 -y remove terra-release; \
        echo "mesa: Terra latest-stable (codec-enabled; repo removed post-bake)" > /usr/lib/apex-mesa-leg; \
    else \
        echo "Terra unavailable — Fedora-stable mesa + RPMFusion freeworld VA"; \
        dnf5 -y install --allowerasing \
            mesa-va-drivers-freeworld mesa-vdpau-drivers-freeworld; \
        echo "mesa: Fedora-stable + freeworld VA (Terra fallback)" > /usr/lib/apex-mesa-leg; \
    fi; \
    rm -f /etc/yum.repos.d/terra*.repo /etc/yum.repos.d/*terra*.repo; \
    cat /usr/lib/apex-mesa-leg; \
    test -z "$(ls /etc/yum.repos.d/ | grep -i terra || true)" \
        || { echo "FATAL: a Terra repo file survived — every later depsolve would abort on its missing gpgkey"; exit 1; }; \
    test -e /usr/lib64/libGLX_mesa.so.0; \
    test -e /usr/lib64/libGLX_nvidia.so.0; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-apps: baked userland (both editions) ──────────────────────────
# The daily-driver application set the plan bakes into every APEX-OS install so a
# fresh boot is usable with zero Flatpak round-trips: Firefox (the default and
# only baked browser), the Thunar + gvfs file-manager stack (yazi, the TUI
# companion, is built in the builder stage), a PDF viewer, image viewer, mpv, the
# archive tools, btop, a calculator, editors (neovim/nano — Zed is fetched below),
# PulseAudio volume control, the disk-management tools, Android mirroring/control,
# and the Bluetooth stack.
# Own transaction, `dnf5 clean all`. bluetooth.service is enabled so BT works on
# first boot; blueman gives the tray applet the shell's BT tab can also drive.
# Package-name notes (verified against Fedora): the Thunar RPM is capitalised
# `Thunar`; Fedora dropped p7zip for `7zip` (upstream 7-Zip, provides 7z + RAR
# extraction); there is no `gvfs-archive` subpackage. `unrar` (RPMFusion nonfree)
# is best-effort — `7zip`/`unar` already cover RAR — so a nonfree-repo hiccup can
# never abort this stage.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y copr enable zeno/scrcpy; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        firefox \
        Thunar thunar-volman thunar-archive-plugin \
        gvfs gvfs-mtp gvfs-smb \
        evince qimgv mpv \
        7zip unzip unar xarchiver \
        btop qalculate-gtk neovim nano \
        alacritty fastfetch \
        pavucontrol \
        gparted parted gdisk udisks2 smartmontools \
        bluez bluez-tools blueman \
        scrcpy android-tools; \
    rpm -q scrcpy android-tools; \
    command -v scrcpy; \
    command -v adb; \
    dnf5 -y copr disable zeno/scrcpy; \
    dnf5 -y install --skip-unavailable unrar; \
    systemctl enable bluetooth.service; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-print: printing + scanning ────────────────────────────────────
# The image had NO print stack at all — no cups, no drivers, no discovery. A
# general-purpose desktop distro that cannot print is not usable by "absolutely
# everyone", and printing is not something a user can bolt on easily on an
# immutable /usr (it needs a system daemon, not a Flatpak).
#
# This is Fedora's @printing group minus cups-browsed: modern CUPS does
# driverless IPP/AirPrint natively via DNS-SD, cups-browsed exists for legacy
# CUPS-broadcast networks, and it carried the 2024 remote-code-execution CVE
# cluster. Left out deliberately; add it if legacy print servers matter to you.
#
# DISCOVERY IS HALF THE JOB. A driverless printer is only findable if mDNS
# works: avahi is already installed AND enabled in this image (verified), but
# `nss-mdns` was absent and /etc/nsswitch.conf had no mdns module, so nothing
# could resolve a printer's .local name.
#
# nss-mdns wires itself through authselect (`%posttrans` runs
# `authselect enable-feature with-mdns4`), which is the ONLY correct mechanism
# here: /etc/nsswitch.conf in this image is a symlink to
# /etc/authselect/nsswitch.conf, and authselect-apply-changes.service is
# enabled — so hand-editing that file would both replace the symlink with a
# regular file and be regenerated away at boot. We re-assert the feature
# idempotently in case the scriptlet is a no-op at build time, then report the
# resulting hosts line. Never fatal: a printer found by IP still prints.
#
# cups.SOCKET, not cups.service: socket activation means cupsd only runs when
# something actually prints, instead of sitting resident on every laptop.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        cups cups-filters cups-pk-helper ghostscript \
        gutenprint gutenprint-cups hplip \
        nss-mdns system-config-printer \
        sane-backends sane-airscan; \
    systemctl enable cups.socket; \
    authselect enable-feature with-mdns4 >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; \
    grep '^hosts:' /etc/nsswitch.conf; \
    grep -q mdns /etc/nsswitch.conf \
        || echo "WARN: mdns not in nsswitch — .local printer discovery degraded"; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-im: input methods (non-Latin scripts) ─────────────────────────
# The image shipped Noto CJK fonts so Chinese/Japanese/Korean text RENDERS,
# but there was no IME anywhere in it — no ibus, no fcitx — so a CJK user
# could read their language and not type a single character of it. That is a
# hard blocker for a very large fraction of "absolutely everyone", and it is
# not fixable post-install on a read-only /usr.
#
# fcitx5 over ibus: this is a wlroots/Hyprland desktop, and fcitx5 is the
# stack with working Wayland text-input-v3 / input-method-v2 support, which is
# what Hyprland implements. Engines cover the major non-Latin input needs:
# chinese-addons (pinyin/shuangpin/table), hangul, anthy (Japanese), and m17n
# (the catch-all: Indic, Cyrillic, Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Vietnamese, …).
#
# ONLY XMODIFIERS is exported. Wayland-native GTK4/Qt6 clients reach fcitx5
# through text-input-v3 with no env vars at all; setting GTK_IM_MODULE/
# QT_IM_MODULE on Wayland is the classic misconfiguration that breaks input in
# native clients. XMODIFIERS is the one that XWayland apps genuinely need, and
# it is inert when fcitx5 is not running.
#
# Autostart is wired in the seeded Hyprland config (files/desktop/hypr/
# hyprland.conf), NOT via /etc/xdg/autostart — greetd execs Hyprland directly,
# so nothing in this image processes XDG autostart entries.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        fcitx5 fcitx5-gtk fcitx5-qt fcitx5-configtool \
        fcitx5-chinese-addons fcitx5-hangul fcitx5-anthy fcitx5-m17n; \
    printf 'XMODIFIERS=@im=fcitx\n' >> /etc/environment; \
    command -v fcitx5 >/dev/null; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-laptop: ordinary laptop hardware every vendor ships ───────────
# Gaps found by diffing the built image against Fedora's @hardware-support and
# the things a normal laptop owner expects to just work:
#
#   fprintd            fingerprint readers (near-universal on business
#                      laptops). Installed but NOT wired into PAM — enabling
#                      the authselect with-fingerprint feature changes the
#                      login stack and is not something to ship untested.
#                      `fprintd-enroll` then works for whoever wants it.
#   iio-sensor-proxy   accelerometer / ambient-light / hinge sensors. Without
#                      it a convertible or tablet has no rotation data at all.
#   switcheroo-control the dGPU-offload service xdg-desktop-portal and
#                      PrefersNonDefaultGPU desktop entries query. On any
#                      hybrid-graphics laptop (a huge share of them) its
#                      absence means apps can never be launched on the dGPU.
#   ModemManager       + NetworkManager-ppp: built-in WWAN/LTE modems, which
#                      is how a lot of business laptops get online at all.
#   NetworkManager-bluetooth  Bluetooth PAN/DUN tethering off a phone.
#   nm-connection-editor  the only GUI here that can configure WPA2-Enterprise,
#                      802.1X, static routes or a VPN — i.e. what a user on a
#                      university or corporate network needs on day one.
#   libinput-utils / usbutils / pciutils  the tools any bug report will ask for.
#
# thermald is Intel-only and its Fedora unit has NO vendor condition, plus
# `Restart=on-failure` — so enabling it as-is would restart-loop into a failed
# unit on every AMD boot. The ExecCondition drop-in makes systemd skip it
# cleanly (condition-failed, not failed) on non-Intel machines, the same
# portability posture Stage 1f applies to the NVIDIA units.
#
# power-profiles-daemon moved here from Containerfile.daily. NOT tlp — tlp
# conflicts with PPD on Fedora and fights the platform profile driver; the plan
# standardises on PPD plus apexd's own sysctl/udev layer. It rides this existing
# transaction rather than getting its own RUN, and that is a download decision,
# not tidiness: every dnf transaction rewrites the ~200 MB sqlite rpmdb into its
# own layer, and in the flavor tier that layer moved on EVERY push and shipped to
# every user on every `apex update`. Here it is inside a layer the base consumes
# by digest and nobody re-downloads.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        fprintd fprintd-pam iio-sensor-proxy switcheroo-control thermald \
        ModemManager NetworkManager-ppp NetworkManager-bluetooth \
        nm-connection-editor power-profiles-daemon \
        libinput-utils usbutils pciutils; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/systemd/system/thermald.service.d; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '# APEX-OS: thermald is Intel-only, its Fedora unit has no vendor gate,' \
        '# and it carries Restart=on-failure — so on an AMD machine it would' \
        '# restart-loop into a permanently failed unit. ExecCondition makes' \
        '# systemd skip it cleanly instead.' \
        '[Service]' \
        'ExecCondition=/bin/sh -c "grep -q GenuineIntel /proc/cpuinfo"' \
        > /usr/lib/systemd/system/thermald.service.d/10-apex-intel-only.conf; \
    systemctl enable switcheroo-control.service; \
    systemctl enable ModemManager.service; \
    systemctl enable thermald.service; \
    systemctl enable power-profiles-daemon.service; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-fonts: full font stack ────────────────────────────────────────
# The base previously shipped only JetBrainsMono Nerd Font, so emoji rendered as
# tofu and CJK was missing entirely. Add Noto Color Emoji + Noto CJK (sans+serif)
# + Noto general sans/serif + the Liberation (MS-metric-compatible) and DejaVu
# families. Rebuild the font cache once. Own transaction, `dnf5 clean all`.
# The gap being fixed is emoji + CJK — keep those (and the always-present
# Liberation/DejaVu Latin families) STRICT. The general-purpose Noto Latin
# families were renamed to variable-font (…-vf-fonts) packages across recent
# Fedora and the legacy names may be gone on F43, so install them best-effort
# (--skip-unavailable, trying both spellings) — Liberation/DejaVu already cover
# Latin regardless.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        google-noto-color-emoji-fonts \
        google-noto-sans-cjk-fonts google-noto-serif-cjk-fonts \
        liberation-sans-fonts liberation-serif-fonts liberation-mono-fonts \
        dejavu-sans-fonts dejavu-serif-fonts dejavu-sans-mono-fonts; \
    dnf5 -y install --skip-unavailable \
        google-noto-sans-vf-fonts google-noto-serif-vf-fonts \
        google-noto-sans-mono-vf-fonts \
        google-noto-sans-fonts google-noto-serif-fonts; \
    fc-cache -f; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-zed: Zed editor (/usr, official tarball) ──────────────────────
# Zed is not packaged for Fedora (upstream ships a Flatpak or a raw tarball, and
# Flatpaks cannot be installed at bootc build time). Fetch the official Linux
# tarball into /usr/lib and symlink the launcher onto PATH. Non-fatal, same
# posture as sing-box: a fetch failure leaves Zed absent (neovim/nano still there)
# but never aborts the image build. The tarball unpacks to `zed.app/`.
RUN set -eux; \
    if curl -fsSL "https://zed.dev/api/releases/stable/latest/zed-linux-x86_64.tar.gz" -o /tmp/zed.tgz \
       && tar -xzf /tmp/zed.tgz -C /usr/lib; then \
        ln -sf /usr/lib/zed.app/bin/zed /usr/bin/zed; \
        install -Dm644 /usr/lib/zed.app/share/applications/zed.desktop \
            /usr/share/applications/dev.zed.Zed.desktop 2>/dev/null || true; \
        echo "zed: installed to /usr/lib/zed.app"; \
    else echo "zed: fetch FAILED (editor absent; non-fatal — neovim/nano remain)"; fi; \
    rm -f /tmp/zed.tgz

# ── Stage 5a-devstack: full development toolchain (apex-logs 35-dev-stack.md) ─
# Both editions ship a complete dev environment out of the box: the C/C++
# toolchain + build systems + debuggers, the language toolchains
# (Python/Node/Rust/Go/Ruby/JDK), and the common CLI dev tools (gh, git-lfs,
# ripgrep, fd, fzf, bat, buildah). rust/cargo exist in the builder stage but not
# the final image, so add them here for users too. Zed (GUI editor) is installed
# in stage 5a-zed above. Own transaction, `dnf5 clean all`.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        gcc gcc-c++ clang llvm lld \
        make automake autoconf libtool pkgconf-pkg-config \
        cmake meson ninja-build gdb strace ltrace \
        python3-pip python3-devel pipx \
        rust cargo golang ruby rubygems \
        java-latest-openjdk-devel \
        nodejs nodejs-npm \
        gh git-lfs ripgrep fd-find fzf bat buildah; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── Stage 5a-claude: Claude Code CLI (apex-logs 35) ─────────────────────────
# System-wide via npm global into /usr (NOT /usr/local — that becomes a symlink
# to writable /var below) so every user gets `claude` on PATH. The autoupdater is
# disabled fleet-wide via /etc/environment (DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1) because it
# cannot write read-only /usr — updates ride the image rebuild. Non-fatal: a
# registry hiccup leaves claude absent but never aborts the build.
#
# HOME=/tmp: in bootc images /root is a symlink to /var/roothome (absent at build
# time), so npm's default cache ($HOME/.npm → /root/.npm) fails with ENOTDIR —
# the same trap the builder stage documents for cargo. Point HOME at a writable
# build dir. Verified: installs `claude` → /usr/bin/claude.
RUN set -eux; \
    export HOME=/tmp; \
    if npm install -g --prefix /usr @anthropic-ai/claude-code; then \
        echo "claude-code: installed ($(claude --version 2>/dev/null || echo present))"; \
    else echo "claude-code: npm install FAILED (absent; non-fatal)"; fi; \
    rm -rf /tmp/.npm /tmp/.node-compile-cache /root/.npm 2>/dev/null || true

# sing-box official static binary (not in Fedora). Shipped DISABLED (unit gates
# on the user's /etc/sing-box/config.json). Non-fatal: a fetch failure leaves the
# VPN tab degraded but never aborts the image build.
RUN set -eux; \
    if ver="$(curl -fsSL https://api.github.com/repos/SagerNet/sing-box/releases/latest | jq -r .tag_name | sed 's/^v//')" \
       && [ -n "$ver" ] \
       && curl -fsSL "https://github.com/SagerNet/sing-box/releases/download/v${ver}/sing-box-${ver}-linux-amd64.tar.gz" -o /tmp/sb.tgz \
       && tar -xzf /tmp/sb.tgz -C /tmp; then \
        install -Dm755 "/tmp/sing-box-${ver}-linux-amd64/sing-box" /usr/bin/sing-box; \
        /usr/bin/sing-box version; echo "sing-box ${ver} installed"; \
    else echo "sing-box: fetch FAILED (VPN tab degraded; non-fatal)"; fi; \
    rm -rf /tmp/sb.tgz /tmp/sing-box-*-linux-amd64

# ── Stage 5b2: zsh + starship as the default interactive shell ─────────────
# zsh with the packaged plugins, plus the modern CLI tools the shipped .zshrc
# aliases to. Every alias is guarded by `command -v`, so the config still works
# in a rescue shell that has none of them — but on a real install they are here.
#
# NO framework (oh-my-zsh etc). A framework is a second config system with its
# own update path and a slow startup; everything APEX needs is a zsh builtin or
# one small package.
#
# fish, nushell and tmux ride in this SAME transaction rather than a new RUN,
# and that is not tidiness: every dnf transaction rewrites the ~200 MB sqlite
# rpmdb into its own layer (the reason the Plymouth engine moved here from the
# flavors, documented below). One transaction instead of two is one rpmdb-sized
# layer instead of two, on the tier where a rebuild already costs the fleet a
# multi-gigabyte update.
#
# Why they are here at all: APEX claims the terminal developer's toolchain is
# "unrestricted and excellent", naming bash/zsh/fish/nushell, tmux/zellij and
# TUI agents. Four of those six were not in any Containerfile — the claim was a
# claim about packages nobody had installed. zellij is not in Fedora and is
# handled separately below.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install \
        zsh zsh-autosuggestions zsh-syntax-highlighting \
        fish nushell tmux \
        eza bat zoxide fzf ripgrep fd-find; \
    dnf5 clean all; \
    # nushell's binary is `nu`, NOT `nushell`. A postcondition that assumed
    # otherwise would fail a build that was fine; one that checked the package
    # name instead of the binary would pass a build that shipped nothing.
    command -v fish; \
    command -v nu; \
    command -v tmux

# starship is NOT packaged in Fedora, and the COPRs offering it are unvetted
# personal repos. Take the official static musl release, pinned by version AND
# verified against its published sha256 — an unverified curl|tar into /usr/bin
# is a supply-chain hole, not a convenience.
ARG STARSHIP_VERSION=v1.26.0
ARG STARSHIP_SHA256=b7c232b0e8249d8e55a40beb79c5c43a7d370f3f9408bd215deb0170daeaadf3
RUN set -eux; \
    url="https://github.com/starship/starship/releases/download/${STARSHIP_VERSION}/starship-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz"; \
    curl -fsSL "$url" -o /tmp/starship.tgz; \
    echo "${STARSHIP_SHA256}  /tmp/starship.tgz" | sha256sum -c -; \
    tar xzf /tmp/starship.tgz -C /usr/bin starship; \
    chmod 0755 /usr/bin/starship; \
    rm -f /tmp/starship.tgz; \
    starship --version

# zellij is likewise NOT packaged in Fedora 43 — `dnf search zellij` returns no
# matches, and naming it in a transaction fails to RESOLVE, taking every other
# package in that transaction down with it. So it gets the starship treatment:
# the official static musl release, pinned by version AND verified against its
# published sha256, because an unverified curl into /usr/bin is a supply-chain
# hole rather than a convenience. The tarball has a single `zellij` member,
# checked with `tar tzf` before this line was written.
#
# Deliberately NOT the `|| echo "fetch FAILED (non-fatal)"` shape the sing-box
# block above uses. sing-box degrades one optional tab; a silently missing
# zellij would reinstate exactly the defect this is closing — a product claim
# naming a program the image does not ship.
ARG ZELLIJ_VERSION=v0.45.1
ARG ZELLIJ_SHA256=40bcc2e03f5d5ae8e054e39f676081fe12ab70871506996ba595834c3718eefc
RUN set -eux; \
    url="https://github.com/zellij-org/zellij/releases/download/${ZELLIJ_VERSION}/zellij-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz"; \
    curl -fsSL "$url" -o /tmp/zellij.tgz; \
    echo "${ZELLIJ_SHA256}  /tmp/zellij.tgz" | sha256sum -c -; \
    tar xzf /tmp/zellij.tgz -C /usr/bin zellij; \
    chmod 0755 /usr/bin/zellij; \
    rm -f /tmp/zellij.tgz; \
    zellij --version

# ── Plymouth engine (theme + default are set in Containerfile.apex) ────────
# The plymouth packages moved here from the retired flavor Containerfiles, and
# the reason is download size, not tidiness. Each flavor ran its own
# `dnf5 -y install plymouth …`, and every dnf transaction rewrites the ~200 MB
# sqlite rpmdb baked into that layer — so both editions shipped an extra
# rpmdb-sized layer that changed on every single build. Installing the engine
# once here leaves the image tier with `plymouth-set-default-theme` + a dracut run,
# neither of which touches the rpmdb. dracut is invoked with --reproducible, so
# an unchanged theme + unchanged kernel now produce a byte-identical initramfs
# and bootc re-fetches nothing.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install plymouth plymouth-scripts plymouth-plugin-script; \
    dnf5 clean all

# ── efibootmgr: an EXPLICIT dependency of the Windows-boot helper ──────────
# The power menu's "Boot into Windows" button (wired up in Containerfile.base)
# is useless without it, and it was only ever present transitively via the
# shim/grub2-efi chain. Made explicit here so a future base change dropping that
# chain cannot silently break the feature. The helper itself, its sudoers rule
# and every assertion about them live in the base tier, because they are
# files/** content.
RUN set -eux; \
    dnf5 -y install efibootmgr; \
    dnf5 clean all; \
    test -x /usr/sbin/efibootmgr -o -x /usr/bin/efibootmgr

# ── npm global prefix: per-user, not root-owned /usr/local (apex-logs 16) ────
# Fedora's nodejs ships /etc/npmrc with `prefix=/usr/local`. On a bootc system
# /usr/local is a symlink to ../var/usrlocal, created root-owned 0755 by the
# tmpfiles rule below, so ANY `npm install -g` as a normal user dies with:
#
#   npm error code EACCES
#   npm error syscall rename
#   npm error path /var/usrlocal/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex
#
# It is not fixable by chmod-ing that directory: anything a user installed after
# a `sudo npm i -g` stays root-owned and the next unprivileged install fails on
# the rename again. And making a shared system path group-writable to install
# arbitrary npm packages is a bad trade.
#
# So point the GLOBAL prefix at the user's own tree. npm expands ${HOME} in
# npmrc (verified against npm 10.9.7: it resolves to /var/home/<user>/.local),
# and ~/.local/bin is already first on PATH via /etc/environment and the seeded
# hyprland.conf, so `npm i -g <pkg>` then `<pkg>` just works with no sudo.
#
# This does NOT affect the image's own globals: the claude-code install above
# passes --prefix /usr explicitly, which overrides npmrc. Anyone who genuinely
# wants a system-wide install can still say `sudo npm i -g --prefix /usr/local`.
#
# Both files are written because ostree keeps the package copy at /usr/etc and
# derives /etc from it; a future nodejs update replaces /usr/etc/npmrc, and this
# RUN re-applies after every dnf install so the fix cannot silently regress.
RUN set -eux; \
    for f in /etc/npmrc /usr/etc/npmrc; do \
        [ -f "$f" ] || continue; \
        sed -i 's|^prefix=/usr/local$|prefix=${HOME}/.local|' "$f"; \
        grep -q '^prefix=\${HOME}/.local$' "$f"; \
        ! grep -q '^prefix=/usr/local$' "$f"; \
    done; \
    grep -q '^prefix=\${HOME}/.local$' /etc/npmrc; \
    echo "npm global prefix -> \${HOME}/.local (was root-owned /usr/local)"

# ── Third-party Rust tools from the builder stage ──────────────────────────
# awww / awww-daemon / matugen / yazi / ya. apexd + apex are NOT here — they are
# the volatile pair and are added by Containerfile.base.
COPY --from=toolbuilder /out/usr/bin/ /usr/bin/

# Wallpaper daemon fallback: awww is built best-effort in the builder stage. If
# it did NOT land (build hiccup), the shell's WallpaperService (`awww img …`) and
# the seeded `exec-once = awww-daemon` would silently do nothing → no wallpaper on
# a fresh install (apex-logs 39). swaybg IS installed, so drop in swaybg-backed
# shims for the two subcommands the shell uses, guaranteeing a working wallpaper
# either way. If the real awww built, these are skipped.
RUN set -eux; \
    if [ ! -x /usr/bin/awww ]; then \
      printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' \
        '# APEX-OS swaybg shim for `awww img … <path>` (real awww absent). apex-logs 39.' \
        'sub="${1:-}"; shift || true' \
        '[ "$sub" = "img" ] || exit 0' \
        'img=""; for a in "$@"; do [ -f "$a" ] && img="$a"; done' \
        '[ -n "$img" ] || exit 0' \
        'pkill -x swaybg >/dev/null 2>&1 || true' \
        'setsid -f swaybg -i "$img" -m fill >/dev/null 2>&1; exit 0' \
        > /usr/bin/awww; \
      printf '%s\n' '#!/usr/bin/env bash' \
        '# APEX-OS swaybg shim for awww-daemon (restore last wallpaper, stay as swaybg). apex-logs 39.' \
        'wall=""; [ -e "$HOME/.curr_wall" ] && wall="$(readlink -f "$HOME/.curr_wall" 2>/dev/null)"' \
        'if [ -z "$wall" ] || [ ! -f "$wall" ]; then wall="/usr/share/backgrounds/apex/default.jpg"; fi' \
        '[ -f "$wall" ] || exit 0' \
        'pkill -x swaybg >/dev/null 2>&1 || true' \
        'exec swaybg -i "$wall" -m fill' \
        > /usr/bin/awww-daemon; \
      chmod 0755 /usr/bin/awww /usr/bin/awww-daemon; \
      echo "awww: real binary absent — installed swaybg shims"; \
    else echo "awww: real binary present"; fi

# ── System defaults + hardening (no dnf) ────────────────────────────────────
#   - os-release: brand as APEX-OS, keep ID=fedora for ecosystem compat; the
#     flavor images append VARIANT/VARIANT_ID after this. (apex-logs 34)
#     Every DISPLAY key is rebranded (NAME/PRETTY_NAME/VERSION/URLs/LOGO/CPE,
#     REDHAT_* dropped). ID and VERSION_ID deliberately stay `fedora`/`43` —
#     they are machine-facing keys with verified consumers: `dnf copr enable`
#     derives its chroot from ID+VERSION_ID (ID=apex → "Chroot not found in the
#     given Copr project (apex-43-x86_64)", reproduced) and dnf's $releasever
#     comes from VERSION_ID. Nothing user-visible reads ID. See docs/branding.md.
#     A surgical `sed -e` (not a heredoc) so a future fedora-bootc base bump
#     keeps any new keys it adds.
#   - /usr/lib/fedora-release: this is what `/etc/system-release` (and
#     /etc/redhat-release) symlink to, and bootupd's get_product_name() reads it,
#     strips / *release.*/ and uses the result as the efibootmgr LABEL. Leaving
#     it as "Fedora release 43 (Forty Three)" is why the firmware boot menu said
#     "Fedora". Content is rewritten in place — the file is NOT renamed, the
#     symlink chain and `[ -f /etc/fedora-release ]` probes must keep working.
#
#     Writing /usr/lib and TRUSTING the /etc symlinks is what broke: every
#     weekly `core` build from 2026-08-17 on failed CI's
#     `grep "^APEX-OS release" /etc/system-release` while /usr/lib/fedora-release
#     itself was branded correctly. fedora-release-common ships those three /etc
#     entries as hardlinked symlinks inherited from the base layer
#     (`/etc/system-release` and `/etc/redhat-release` share one inode, nlink=3);
#     nothing in this file, and no package in any repo we enable, owns or
#     rewrites them — verified with `dnf5 repoquery --whatprovides`, which names
#     only fedora-release-common and generic-release-common.
#
#     What moved was the BUILDER. GitHub's ubuntu-24.04 runner image
#     20260810.271 (published 2026-08-11) replaced the distro podman 4.9.3 with
#     a bundled 5.8.4 — precisely the gap between the last green scheduled core
#     (2026-08-03, image 20260720.247) and the first red one (2026-08-17, image
#     20260810.271.1). GitHub withdrew it again in 20260831.293, citing
#     "unexpected container storage behavior and reports of corrupted images"
#     and "conflicts between the bundled and distribution-provided Podman
#     installations" (actions/runner-images#14642). An /etc entry that this
#     image only ever INHERITS and never writes is at the mercy of whatever the
#     day's builder does when it flattens 45 layers.
#
#     That rollback also means the cron may go green on its own — which is the
#     reason to fix it properly rather than wait: nothing here would have
#     changed, and the next builder that mishandles an inherited symlink would
#     ship a firmware boot entry labelled "Fedora" all over again.
#
#     So re-create the links here instead of assuming they survived. `ln -sfn`
#     is a no-op against the correct state, and against a flattened one it puts
#     a fresh symlink in THIS layer — written after the content it points at,
#     by us, rather than carried through 40 layers of somebody else's copy-up.
#     ALL SEVEN are re-created, not just the one CI happened to check: every
#     file this block brands lives in /usr/lib and is only ever read through
#     /etc, so os-release, the release trio, the CPE and issue{,.net} are the
#     same bug waiting for a different symptom. A flattened /etc/os-release is
#     the worst of them — /usr/lib/os-release would still pass every assertion
#     while the whole system read Fedora's.
#
#     Then assert through /etc, which is the side that is actually consumed.
#     This block checked /usr/lib/os-release three ways and checked nothing at
#     all through /etc, which is why a wrong image built cleanly and only died
#     45 minutes later in the CI verify — and only on the Monday cron, because
#     push builds skip this tier. The verify check stays: it is the backstop
#     for anything that goes wrong AFTER this RUN commits, which is exactly the
#     class of bug that caused this.
#   - /usr/lib/issue{,.net}: agetty expands \S to PRETTY_NAME (so the VT login
#     banner brands itself), but the stock "Kernel \r on \m" line prints
#     7.1.3-cachyos1.fc43.x86_64. Drop that line — the kernel release string
#     itself cannot be changed without building the kernel from source.
#   - LOGO=apex-os-logo names an icon that is COPY'd in the base tier, so the
#     hicolor cache rebuild lives there too — running it here would cache an
#     icon theme that does not yet contain the spark. Same for `dconf update`,
#     which compiles a database seeded from files/system/dconf/.
#   - mask serial-getty@ttyS0: the console=ttyS0 karg (for QEMU/CI) makes systemd
#     spawn a getty that restart-loops on hardware with no serial port; masking
#     stops the loop while serial *console* output still works. (apex-logs 04)
#   - mask systemd-remount-fs: fails on the composefs/overlay root. (apex-logs 03)
#   - /usr/local -> ../var/usrlocal: /usr is read-only on bootc, so redirect
#     /usr/local to writable per-machine state + a tmpfiles dir. (apex-logs 08)
#   - /etc/environment: session-wide theme + default terminal + Claude autoupdate
#     off, via pam_env so a greetd/Hyprland session (no uwsm) inherits them.
#   - /etc/bashrc: fastfetch greeting on every interactive terminal, colour by
#     scheme, once per shell session. (apex-logs 33) The greeting script itself
#     is COPY'd in the base tier; the source line is guarded with `-r`, so it is
#     inert until that lands and correct afterwards.
RUN set -eux; \
    sed -i \
      -e 's|^NAME=.*|NAME="APEX-OS"|' \
      -e 's|^PRETTY_NAME=.*|PRETTY_NAME="APEX-OS"|' \
      -e 's|^VERSION=.*|VERSION="43"|' \
      -e 's|^VERSION_CODENAME=.*|VERSION_CODENAME=""|' \
      -e 's|^ANSI_COLOR=.*|ANSI_COLOR="0;38;2;161;201;153"|' \
      -e 's|^LOGO=.*|LOGO=apex-os-logo|' \
      -e 's|^CPE_NAME=.*|CPE_NAME="cpe:/o:apexos:apex_os:43"|' \
      -e 's|^HOME_URL=.*|HOME_URL="https://github.com/AndreNijman/apex-os"|' \
      -e 's|^DOCUMENTATION_URL=.*|DOCUMENTATION_URL="https://github.com/AndreNijman/apex-os#readme"|' \
      -e 's|^SUPPORT_URL=.*|SUPPORT_URL="https://github.com/AndreNijman/apex-os/discussions"|' \
      -e 's|^BUG_REPORT_URL=.*|BUG_REPORT_URL="https://github.com/AndreNijman/apex-os/issues"|' \
      -e 's|^DEFAULT_HOSTNAME=.*|DEFAULT_HOSTNAME="apex"|' \
      -e '/^REDHAT_/d' \
      /usr/lib/os-release; \
    grep -q '^PRETTY_NAME="APEX-OS"$' /usr/lib/os-release; \
    test "$(grep -ci fedora /usr/lib/os-release)" = 1; \
    grep -qx 'ID=fedora' /usr/lib/os-release; \
    printf '%s\n' 'APEX-OS release 43' > /usr/lib/fedora-release; \
    printf '%s\n' 'cpe:/o:apexos:apex_os:43' > /usr/lib/system-release-cpe; \
    printf '%s\n\n' '\S' > /usr/lib/issue; \
    printf '%s\n' '\S' > /usr/lib/issue.net; \
    ln -sfn ../usr/lib/os-release         /etc/os-release; \
    ln -sfn ../usr/lib/fedora-release     /etc/fedora-release; \
    ln -sfn fedora-release                /etc/system-release; \
    ln -sfn fedora-release                /etc/redhat-release; \
    ln -sfn ../usr/lib/system-release-cpe /etc/system-release-cpe; \
    ln -sfn ../usr/lib/issue              /etc/issue; \
    ln -sfn ../usr/lib/issue.net          /etc/issue.net; \
    grep -q '^APEX-OS release' /etc/system-release; \
    grep -q '^APEX-OS release' /etc/redhat-release; \
    grep -q '^APEX-OS release' /etc/fedora-release; \
    grep -q '^PRETTY_NAME="APEX-OS"$' /etc/os-release; \
    grep -qx 'cpe:/o:apexos:apex_os:43' /etc/system-release-cpe; \
    systemctl mask serial-getty@ttyS0.service; \
    systemctl mask systemd-remount-fs.service; \
    rm -rf /usr/local && ln -s ../var/usrlocal /usr/local; \
    printf 'd /var/usrlocal 0755 root root -\n' > /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/apex-usrlocal.conf; \
    printf 'QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=qt6ct\nTERMINAL=alacritty\nDISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1\n' >> /etc/environment; \
    printf '\n# APEX-OS fastfetch greeting. The logic lives in ONE file shared with zsh —\n# zsh is the default login shell, so a bash-only greeting was never seen by\n# real users (apex-logs 14). Guarded so a partial image cannot break bash.\n[ -r /usr/share/apex/shell/greeting.sh ] && . /usr/share/apex/shell/greeting.sh\n' >> /etc/bashrc

# ── zsh becomes the default login shell for accounts created from here on ──
# This sets the DEFAULT in useradd's config rather than rewriting existing
# accounts — changing a shell out from under an existing user is not ours to do.
# The shipped zshrc/starship templates and every assertion about them live in
# the base tier (they are files/** COPYs).
RUN set -eux; \
    sed -i 's|^SHELL=.*|SHELL=/bin/zsh|' /etc/default/useradd; \
    grep -q '^SHELL=/bin/zsh' /etc/default/useradd; \
    grep -q '^/bin/zsh$' /etc/shells || echo /bin/zsh >> /etc/shells; \
    # Installed is not the same as usable. `chsh` refuses a shell that is not
    # listed in /etc/shells when a non-root user runs it, so an unlisted shell
    # is one a user cannot actually switch to. fish and tmux add themselves
    # from their own %post scriptlets (both write the /usr/bin and /bin forms);
    # nushell's package does not, so APEX registers it.
    for s in /usr/bin/nu /bin/nu; do \
        grep -qx "$s" /etc/shells || echo "$s" >> /etc/shells; \
    done; \
    # Assert the scriptlet-registered entries actually landed. Checking only
    # the lines written above would never notice a scriptlet that silently did
    # not run, which is the failure mode worth catching here.
    grep -qx /usr/bin/fish /etc/shells; \
    grep -qx /usr/bin/tmux /etc/shells; \
    grep -qx /usr/bin/nu /etc/shells

# ── NO unattended OS updates, and NO unattended reboots ─────────────────────
# fedora-bootc ships bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer, which on this machine was
# ACTIVE despite being `preset: disabled` — nothing in this repo enabled it. It
# fires roughly every 2h, runs `bootc upgrade --apply`, and then reboots the
# machine on its own. Observed live (apex-logs 57):
#
#   systemd[1]: Stopped run-p147322-i134392.service - [systemd-run]
#               /usr/bin/systemctl reboot "--message=Initiated by bootc"
#
# It rebooted a working desktop mid-session, with no prompt and no warning, to
# pick up an image that had been pushed minutes earlier. On a server that is a
# reasonable default. On a daily-driver laptop it is data loss waiting to happen:
# anything unsaved in any application is simply gone.
#
# APEX-OS already has an explicit, user-driven update path — `apex update`
# (bootc upgrade + fwupdmgr) — plus `apex rollback` and `apex pin`. An
# unattended timer duplicating that while adding a surprise reboot is strictly
# worse than the command the user chose to run.
#
# Masked rather than disabled: `disabled` was not enough here (it was disabled
# AND active). Masking is the only state that cannot be silently re-activated by
# a preset or a dependency. It lands in /etc, so it is 3-way-merged and any user
# who WANTS unattended updates can opt back in with:
#
#     sudo systemctl unmask bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer
#     sudo systemctl enable --now bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer
#
# Updates are still fully available — they are just always the user's decision.
RUN set -eux; \
    systemctl mask bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer; \
    systemctl mask bootc-fetch-apply-updates.service; \
    test -L /etc/systemd/system/bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer; \
    test "$(readlink /etc/systemd/system/bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer)" = /dev/null; \
    echo "unattended bootc updates masked - 'apex update' is the update path"

# ── Default locale: a REAL one, not systemd's C.UTF-8 fallback ──────────────
# With no /etc/locale.conf, systemd falls back to LANG=C.UTF-8, which has no
# collation rules (bytewise sorting) and C-style time/number formatting. Nothing
# in the image set one, and the script installer (installer/apex-install) never
# ran localectl either — so every install booted with the fallback. Shipping the
# file here means EVERY install path gets a valid locale, including a bare
# `bootc install` with no APEX installer involved.
#
# This is an /etc file, so ostree 3-way-merges it: a user who runs
# `localectl set-locale ...` overrides it and their choice survives upgrades.
# Requires glibc-langpack-en (installed in the desktop-stack layer above) —
# without it this would name a locale that does not exist. See apex-logs 53.
RUN printf '# APEX-OS default locale. Override with: localectl set-locale LANG=...\nLANG=en_US.UTF-8\n' \
      > /etc/locale.conf; \
    printf '# APEX-OS default console keymap. Override with: localectl set-keymap ...\nKEYMAP=us\n' \
      > /etc/vconsole.conf

# ── Default timezone ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /etc/localtime did not exist at all on the p6 install, so systemd fell back to
# UTC: the clock read 13:45 while the wall clock said 21:45 (AWST is UTC+8).
# Everything time-stamped was eight hours out — the bar clock, journal entries,
# file mtimes, hypridle's timers. Neither the image nor installer/apex-install
# ever wrote it; only the ISO kickstart did (`timezone Australia/Perth --utc`),
# and this machine was installed via the script path.
#
# Australia/Perth matches installer/bib-config.toml so both install paths agree.
# It is an /etc symlink, so ostree 3-way-merges it and
# `timedatectl set-timezone <tz>` overrides it permanently.
# See apex-logs 55 / apexlogs/09-timezone-utc.md.
RUN ln -sfn ../usr/share/zoneinfo/Australia/Perth /etc/localtime

# ── Container sanity lint (informational) ─────────────────────────────────
RUN bootc container lint || true
